{:edb/db-before-t 2558 :edb/db-after-t 2559 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535871 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:29:54.988Z" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124255 1100 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A$?\n\n1. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n2. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n3. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124255 1100 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124255 1145 "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A$. As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A$. Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A$. Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124255 1145 "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124256 1100 "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n2. $B +4I$ is singular\n3. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124256 1100 "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\n- $B +4I$ is singular\n\n- $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124256 1145 "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M$, we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n> $M$ is invertible $Col(M) = R^{4}; dim(Col(M)) = 4 = rank(M); Null(M) = {0}; dim(Null(M)) = 0 = nullity(M)$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem,\n$nullity(B + 4I)|= 4 - rank(B + 4I); = 4 - 3; = 1$.\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0$, we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B$. As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124256 1145 "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/assets/6273b311b461cc16f516a4d9da6d928643e988c7847cfd14e23401a265d9e25b.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124335 1100 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n2. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n3. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124335 1100 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124335 1145 "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M$, we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n> $M$ is invertible $Col(M) = R^{3}; dim(Col(M)) = 3 = rank(M); Null(M) = {0}; dim(Null(M)) = 0 = nullity(M)$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I$.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A$, which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n$\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n$\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124335 1145 "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/assets/fd48d2627c161d3e82529aeeb42bcd830c2fc83a9c8d0b7522d1857f099d6f38.png)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124449 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371505 13194139535871 true] [17592186124449 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371512 13194139535871 true] [17592186124449 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371533 13194139535871 true] [17592186124449 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371540 13194139535871 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371526 13194139535871 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371568 13194139535871 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371575 13194139535871 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371582 13194139535871 true] [17592186124450 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371589 13194139535871 true] [17592186124526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371519 13194139535871 true] [17592186124526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371547 13194139535871 true] [17592186124526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371554 13194139535871 true] [17592186124526 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371561 13194139535871 true] [17592186124992 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535871 true] [17592186124992 1100 "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" 13194139535871 true] [17592186124992 1100 "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $λ^{3} - λ^{2} - 6λ = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $λ = 3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n2. $λ = 0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n3. $A$ is invertible" 13194139535871 false] [17592186124992 1145 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186125081 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535871 true] [17592186125081 1100 "If $A$ is a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix and $λ = 3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(A - 3I)x = 0$ has a nonzero solution\n2. $Col(A - 3I) = R^{2}$\n3. $nullity(A - 3I) = 0$" 13194139535871 false] [17592186125081 1100 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" 13194139535871 true] [17592186125081 1145 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371505 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371506 13194139535871 true] [17592186371505 1089 "q-140834" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371505 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535871 true] [17592186371505 1100 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371505 1113 #uuid "13dbff1b-f209-56fa-97e5-9685250b7352" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371505 1145 "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371507 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371508 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371509 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371510 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371511 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1041 "selection" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371511 13194139535871 true] [17592186371506 1110 #uuid "23355173-e3d6-5c2e-8233-b0a03f1524cd" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371507 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371507 1111 #uuid "7f1ffa3e-f8a4-55f3-aeac-0795ae9f9ad4" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371508 1034 "I only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371508 1111 #uuid "84509c98-f383-5fef-80d4-fa06513e4d9a" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371509 1034 "III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371509 1111 #uuid "ecc37c78-13b5-509e-bb2e-25c08f2d2448" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371510 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371510 1111 #uuid "708bfd5c-b140-52ff-8eb3-1001463e8221" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371511 1034 "II only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371511 1111 #uuid "87281ea9-47aa-5b26-bb1b-750bda068338" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371512 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371513 13194139535871 true] [17592186371512 1089 "q-140835" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371512 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535871 true] [17592186371512 1100 "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371512 1113 #uuid "2f116f8b-9ad9-560d-9353-a5ae00fb693d" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371512 1145 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371514 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371515 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371516 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371517 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371518 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1041 "selection" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371513 1104 #edb/ref 17592