" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371564 1111 #uuid "ae79dc86-a664-590c-b1b9-670e9e1abfbd" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371565 1034 "III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371565 1111 #uuid "60f3e6a6-8655-5b60-9a70-a8587f1955b6" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371566 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371566 1111 #uuid "f0af26a0-a817-5ac8-9d1d-6b11d6565dc3" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371567 1034 "II only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371567 1111 #uuid "80711d17-2848-5c34-b142-eb70572e0d4a" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371568 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371569 13194139535871 true] [17592186371568 1089 "q-97962" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371568 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535871 true] [17592186371568 1100 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}$\n- $A-3I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371568 1113 #uuid "7e96e49f-d26f-5fc8-b00b-d17cc89b1991" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371568 1145 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A-3I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A-3I) \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is non-singular, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371570 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371571 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371572 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371573 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371574 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1041 "selection" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371572 13194139535871 true] [17592186371569 1110 #uuid "df675f47-c548-58d2-a400-5654b845da98" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371570 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371570 1111 #uuid "5ea2a7a2-d9fb-5c2b-b936-17cf236576e1" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371571 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371571 1111 #uuid "23a8e9ff-b2b1-5d14-b67d-4b039c444461" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371572 1034 "I only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371572 1111 #uuid "425e462a-9222-5f1b-bf0c-708a8337ec08" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371573 1034 "III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371573 1111 #uuid "8f267ff6-c3b6-5655-8242-e2fa8c874a89" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371574 1034 "II only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 131941