5 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371584 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371585 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371586 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371587 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371588 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1041 "selection" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371587 13194139535871 true] [17592186371583 1110 #uuid "5c7d216d-4421-51c1-943f-2412252a6f9d" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371584 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535871 true] [17592186371584 1111 #uuid "53589411-7e05-5ed6-8a4f-2c38ea39c75d" 13194139535871 true] [17592186371585 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535871 true] [